most $9$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ has at most two non-negative distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfba6eb1-d0b6-5cbc-ac16-d682a2e1cc93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493969 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $6\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $8\\times 9$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $9\\times 8$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $9\\times 9$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{9\\times 8}\\times {A}_{8\\times 9}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{9\\times 9}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $9$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186508120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186508123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186508139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186508145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186508146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing the Singular Values of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186508145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507599 #edb/ref 1049 